WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
Earth is closest to the Sun in early January.

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.740
Yet January is winter in the north.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.540
So distance does not make the seasons.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.300
Earth's axis is tilted about 23.4 degrees.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.640
Through the year, each hemisphere in turn leans toward the Sun, and gets more direct

6
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.060
light and longer days.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:31.050
Shine a torch straight down at a table and you get a small bright spot.

8
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.470
Tilt it, and the same light covers more ground.

9
00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:41.510
At the top of the atmosphere, a surface facing the Sun gets about 1,360 watts.

10
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.490
That is per square metre.

11
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.830
Put the Sun 60 degrees from overhead, and a flat surface gets half of that.

12
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:52.623
Now spread that light over the whole planet.

13
00:00:53.503 --> 00:00:55.603
Earth intercepts sunlight on a disk.

14
00:00:56.023 --> 00:00:58.183
But it spreads that light over a sphere.

15
00:00:59.143 --> 00:01:01.843
A sphere has four times the area of the disk.

16
00:01:02.403 --> 00:01:08.683
So the global mean at the top of the atmosphere is 1,360 divided by 4.

17
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:12.063
That is 340 watts per square metre.

18
00:01:12.843 --> 00:01:15.503
The tropics get more, and the poles get less.

19
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:19.313
That uneven heating moves the air.

20
00:01:20.073 --> 00:01:23.133
Near the equator, warm moist air rises.

21
00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:28.393
As it rises it cools, its water vapour condenses, and it rains.

22
00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:33.793
The dry air flows poleward at height and sinks in the subtropics.

23
00:01:34.613 --> 00:01:38.433
Sinking air is compressed and warms, so rain is suppressed.

24
00:01:39.193 --> 00:01:42.893
The Sahara and the Kalahari lie under this sinking air.

25
00:01:43.593 --> 00:01:47.013
This loop is the Hadley cell, an idealized model.

26
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:54.413
A Walter-Lieth diagram puts monthly temperature and rainfall on one chart, with 10

27
00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:56.733
degrees level with 20 millimetres.

28
00:01:57.573 --> 00:02:01.693
Take a made-up lowland station at 26 degrees all year.

29
00:02:02.293 --> 00:02:05.153
That temperature plots at 52 millimetres.

30
00:02:05.873 --> 00:02:11.833
Its driest month, September, has 90 millimetres, above that line, so there is no dry

31
00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:17.713
period. The Köppen-Geiger rules call it Af, a tropical rainforest climate.

32
00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:24.067
That match is a hypothesis to test against the vegetation actually present.

33
00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:30.207
Staver and colleagues analysed tree cover across the tropics of Africa, Australia

34
00:02:30.607 --> 00:02:31.607
and South America.

35
00:02:32.427 --> 00:02:38.147
Take yearly rain between about 1,000 and 2,500 millimetres, with a dry season

36
00:02:38.147 --> 00:02:39.227
under seven months.

37
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.987
There, places were mostly savanna or forest.

38
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:46.567
Fire was associated with keeping areas open.

39
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:51.787
Savanna and forest may be alternative states under the same climate.

40
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:54.507
Climate predicts a biome roughly.

41
00:02:55.047 --> 00:02:56.047
It does not fix it.
